1
00:00:01,376 --> 00:00:02,127
Oh, hi.
Hey.

2
00:00:02,169 --> 00:00:03,420
Hey.

3
00:00:03,462 --> 00:00:04,964
So, uh, what's the big news

4
00:00:05,005 --> 00:00:07,299
you had us rush
all the way over here for?

5
00:00:07,341 --> 00:00:09,635
Okay, our news...
my company has asked me

6
00:00:09,677 --> 00:00:11,345
to head up our office
in Tulsa.

7
00:00:11,386 --> 00:00:14,264
So, as of Monday,
I'm being officially relocated.

8
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:16,099
What?
Monday?
Oh, my God.

9
00:00:16,141 --> 00:00:17,685
Well, how long do
you have to go for?

10
00:00:17,726 --> 00:00:19,603
Well, they said it could be
up to a year.

11
00:00:19,645 --> 00:00:20,646
A year?
Oh, my God.

12
00:00:20,688 --> 00:00:22,230
Do you have to go?

13
00:00:24,441 --> 00:00:26,902
Well, I kind of
have to, don't I?

14
00:00:26,944 --> 00:00:29,154
'Cause of this stupid thing.

15
00:00:33,074 --> 00:00:36,411
There's nothing like the support
of your loving wife, huh?

16
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:38,747
Wait a minute, wait...
you can't go to Tulsa.

17
00:00:38,789 --> 00:00:40,708
Maybe you forgot,
but we have tickets

18
00:00:40,749 --> 00:00:42,167
to the Jets game next week.

19
00:00:42,209 --> 00:00:43,961
Well, I'm sorry, buddy

20
00:00:44,003 --> 00:00:46,005
but I don't think
I'm going to be
able to make it.

21
00:00:46,045 --> 00:00:49,383
W-W-We were going to go
see the Jets.

22
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:52,553
You-You can't go.

23
00:00:52,594 --> 00:00:55,556
I mean, you're the glue
that holds this group together.

24
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:56,724
Really?

25
00:00:56,765 --> 00:00:58,517
Not you.

26
00:01:00,435 --> 00:01:04,105
God, I can't believe
you guys are moving.

27
00:01:06,692 --> 00:01:07,902
I call their apartment!

28
00:01:07,943 --> 00:01:09,110
Oh, no.
No.

29
00:01:09,152 --> 00:01:11,154
Yeah!
Come on.

30
00:01:16,451 --> 00:01:21,415
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

31
00:01:21,456 --> 00:01:23,542
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

32
00:01:23,584 --> 00:01:26,127
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

33
00:01:26,169 --> 00:01:30,966
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

34
00:01:31,008 --> 00:01:33,301
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

35
00:01:33,343 --> 00:01:35,846
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

36
00:01:35,888 --> 00:01:40,517
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

37
00:01:40,559 --> 00:01:45,564
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

38
00:01:48,776 --> 00:01:49,818
Here you go, Rach.

39
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:50,903
Oh, thank you, Joey.

40
00:01:50,945 --> 00:01:52,112
Oh, you know what?

41
00:01:52,153 --> 00:01:54,573
I'm not even sure
I can have caffeine.

42
00:01:54,615 --> 00:01:56,617
I went through this
with Ben and Carol.

43
00:01:56,658 --> 00:01:58,786
One cup of coffee
won't affect your milk.

44
00:01:58,827 --> 00:02:01,747
Yeah, well, just to be sure,
I'm going to call Dr. Wiener.

45
00:02:01,789 --> 00:02:04,332
( chuckling )

46
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:07,210
Every time?

47
00:02:07,252 --> 00:02:09,379
Uh-huh.

48
00:02:09,421 --> 00:02:11,006
Rach, you don't
have to call him

49
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:13,092
whenever you have
a little question, okay?

50
00:02:13,132 --> 00:02:14,259
Trust me. I know this.

51
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:16,053
All right, I trust you.

52
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:19,431
Rachel, I can see you dialing.

53
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:20,724
I don't understand why...

54
00:02:20,766 --> 00:02:22,476
Shh, I'm on the phone.
Dr. Wiener?

55
00:02:22,517 --> 00:02:24,561
( chuckles )

56
00:02:26,021 --> 00:02:28,732
It's so weird seeing Ross
and Rachel with a baby.

57
00:02:28,774 --> 00:02:29,984
It's just so grown up.

58
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:31,318
I know, yeah.

59
00:02:31,359 --> 00:02:33,612
I feel like
we're all growing up.

60
00:02:33,654 --> 00:02:34,613
Uh-huh.

61
00:02:34,655 --> 00:02:36,115
A person named Wiener.

62
00:02:36,156 --> 00:02:37,365
God that kills me.

63
00:02:37,407 --> 00:02:39,827
Look at you all grown up.

64
00:02:39,868 --> 00:02:42,537
Yeah, well, actually--
actually, you know what?

65
00:02:42,579 --> 00:02:44,372
I am.

66
00:02:44,414 --> 00:02:45,624
You know...
Yeah.

67
00:02:45,666 --> 00:02:47,417
Yeah, well,
that whole thing with Rachel

68
00:02:47,459 --> 00:02:49,252
made me realize
that maybe I'm ready

69
00:02:49,294 --> 00:02:51,421
for a more serious
relationship, you know?

70
00:02:51,463 --> 00:02:55,092
Like, I'd like to meet a nice,
mature, commitment-minded lady.

71
00:02:55,134 --> 00:02:57,136
And looks aren't important as...

72
00:02:58,512 --> 00:03:00,263
Nah, she's got to be hot.

73
00:03:00,305 --> 00:03:02,474
Well, you know, I
might know somebody.

74
00:03:02,516 --> 00:03:05,060
Hey, how about
you setting me
up with someone

75
00:03:05,102 --> 00:03:06,227
and we double-date?

76
00:03:06,269 --> 00:03:07,270
I can do that.

77
00:03:07,312 --> 00:03:08,730
Yeah, how's Friday?

78
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:09,815
Done.
All right.

79
00:03:09,857 --> 00:03:11,108
Oh, good, really?
Yeah.

80
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:12,233
Okay, let's see...

81
00:03:12,275 --> 00:03:13,568
( little-girl giggle )

82
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:15,821
All right... um...

83
00:03:15,863 --> 00:03:18,490
ooh, you know who's great?
Sandy Poopack.

84
00:03:18,532 --> 00:03:19,950
( chuckles )

85
00:03:19,992 --> 00:03:21,994
Poo-pack.

86
00:03:24,038 --> 00:03:25,372
Yeah, all right.

87
00:03:25,413 --> 00:03:27,206
Well, that rules
out Lana Titwiller.

88
00:03:27,248 --> 00:03:29,626
( laughing )

89
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:33,338
Hey.
Hey.

90
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:34,715
I've got good news.

91
00:03:34,756 --> 00:03:36,175
You got out
of the whole Tulsa thing?

92
00:03:36,215 --> 00:03:38,760
Okay, I have news.

93
00:03:40,345 --> 00:03:42,221
You don't have to move to Tulsa.

94
00:03:42,263 --> 00:03:44,349
You can stay here
and keep your job.

95
00:03:44,391 --> 00:03:45,976
That's great. How?

96
00:03:46,018 --> 00:03:47,644
Well, my boss and I
worked out a deal

97
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:49,938
where I only have to be
in Tulsa four days a week.

98
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:51,899
So, the other three
I can be here with you.

99
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:55,610
So, you're going to be gone
four days a week?

100
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:57,821
No.

101
00:03:57,863 --> 00:04:01,241
I'm sorry, are you just
used to saying that?

102
00:04:03,326 --> 00:04:06,747
No. I can't be away
from you for that long.

103
00:04:06,788 --> 00:04:07,748
Really?

104
00:04:07,789 --> 00:04:09,374
Yeah, you're my husband.

105
00:04:09,416 --> 00:04:11,877
I'm not going to live in
a different state than you

106
00:04:11,919 --> 00:04:13,545
for 208 days out of the year.

107
00:04:13,587 --> 00:04:16,423
That's fast math.

108
00:04:16,464 --> 00:04:18,842
We could use you in Tulsa.

109
00:04:18,884 --> 00:04:22,054
Honey, thanks for trying
to figure out a way

110
00:04:22,096 --> 00:04:24,056
but if you're
going to Tulsa

111
00:04:24,098 --> 00:04:25,724
I want to go with you.

112
00:04:25,766 --> 00:04:28,143
Hey, you said that
without gagging.

113
00:04:28,185 --> 00:04:29,561
I know.

114
00:04:34,024 --> 00:04:35,859
Well, excuse me?

115
00:04:35,901 --> 00:04:36,902
Oh, yeah?

116
00:04:36,944 --> 00:04:38,570
Well, up yours, too.

117
00:04:40,739 --> 00:04:41,782
Who the hell was that?

118
00:04:41,823 --> 00:04:43,617
Dr. Wiener.

119
00:04:45,911 --> 00:04:49,123
Rach, you can't call people
at 3:00 in the morning.

120
00:04:49,164 --> 00:04:50,749
You know what?
You sound just like his wife.

121
00:04:52,751 --> 00:04:54,711
Was there anything
even wrong with Emma?

122
00:04:54,753 --> 00:04:56,838
Yes, of course
there is.

123
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:59,382
Okay, I'm not insane.

124
00:04:59,424 --> 00:05:01,093
Well, what was it?

125
00:05:01,135 --> 00:05:03,929
Hiccups.

126
00:05:03,971 --> 00:05:06,098
Rach, I told you,
you can't call him

127
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,391
every time any little thing
comes up.

128
00:05:08,433 --> 00:05:11,352
Yeah, well, not anymore I can't.

129
00:05:11,394 --> 00:05:12,562
He fired us.

130
00:05:14,106 --> 00:05:15,607
God, what are we
going to do?

131
00:05:15,649 --> 00:05:17,317
We have to find
a pediatrician.

132
00:05:17,358 --> 00:05:18,693
Wait, wait, wait.

133
00:05:18,735 --> 00:05:20,279
Monica said that when you guys
were growing up

134
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:21,446
you really liked
your doctor.

135
00:05:21,488 --> 00:05:22,530
What was his name?

136
00:05:22,572 --> 00:05:25,075
Dr. Gettleman?

137
00:05:25,117 --> 00:05:26,367
Uh, yeah, no, I don't...

138
00:05:26,409 --> 00:05:28,120
I don't think
that's a good idea.

139
00:05:28,162 --> 00:05:30,289
In fact I think he's--
I think he's dead.

140
00:05:30,330 --> 00:05:32,457
Ach, why does everything
happen to me?

141
00:05:32,499 --> 00:05:33,834
Look, Rach, I promise

142
00:05:33,875 --> 00:05:36,295
first thing tomorrow
we'll find another doctor

143
00:05:36,335 --> 00:05:37,754
but I got to get up early

144
00:05:37,796 --> 00:05:39,673
and I'm not feeling
all that well.

145
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:41,340
What? Wh-Wh-What
you're not feeling well?

146
00:05:41,382 --> 00:05:43,051
What do you have?
Is it rubella?

147
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:45,012
Because don't go near Emma,
she has not had that shot.

148
00:05:45,053 --> 00:05:49,266
You know, come to think of it,
it does feel rubella-like.

149
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:02,863
Weeennneeer!

150
00:06:02,904 --> 00:06:03,864
Wiener!

151
00:06:03,905 --> 00:06:05,490
Wiener, Wiener, Wiener...

152
00:06:05,532 --> 00:06:06,908
Rachel!

153
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:09,870
Great. Now he's going to know
it was me.

154
00:06:15,209 --> 00:06:16,584
Hey.
Hey.

155
00:06:16,626 --> 00:06:18,753
So, how is this for our
big double-date tonight?

156
00:06:21,006 --> 00:06:22,966
Oh, my God.

157
00:06:23,008 --> 00:06:24,301
Ooh, great.

158
00:06:24,343 --> 00:06:26,427
Just the reaction
I was hoping for.

159
00:06:26,469 --> 00:06:30,140
Yeah, yes, so you, uh,
you found someone for me?

160
00:06:30,182 --> 00:06:31,558
You didn't forget?

161
00:06:31,599 --> 00:06:32,934
Of course not.

162
00:06:32,976 --> 00:06:34,435
And you are going
to love Mary Ellen.

163
00:06:34,477 --> 00:06:37,605
She's really smart and
cute and funny and...

164
00:06:37,647 --> 00:06:39,440
I can't tell you
how I know this

165
00:06:39,482 --> 00:06:41,568
but she is not opposed
to threesomes.

166
00:06:41,609 --> 00:06:42,903
Oh, really?

167
00:06:42,944 --> 00:06:44,238
( mumbles )

168
00:06:44,279 --> 00:06:45,739
Yeah.

169
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:47,824
So, tell me something
about my guy.

170
00:06:47,866 --> 00:06:49,659
No.

171
00:06:51,203 --> 00:06:53,580
Come on, give me something.

172
00:06:53,621 --> 00:06:55,040
What's his name?

173
00:06:55,082 --> 00:06:56,833
Mike.

174
00:06:56,875 --> 00:06:57,834
Mike?
Yeah.

175
00:06:57,876 --> 00:06:58,919
Okay. What's his last name?

176
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:00,419
Damn it, is there no mystery

177
00:07:00,461 --> 00:07:01,713
left in romance anymore?

178
00:07:01,755 --> 00:07:03,715
All right, we'll
see you and Mike

179
00:07:03,757 --> 00:07:06,009
at the restaurant
in couple of hours.

180
00:07:06,051 --> 00:07:07,468
Okay. All right, great.

181
00:07:07,510 --> 00:07:09,679
All right. Yeah.
See you. Bye-bye.

182
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,099
Why did I have to say Mike?

183
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:13,558
I don't know a Mike.

184
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:15,602
Why couldn't I have said...

185
00:07:17,645 --> 00:07:19,231
There's no guys in there.

186
00:07:22,483 --> 00:07:25,404
Oh, it's impossible
to find a good doctor.

187
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:27,906
I mean, how do you know
the good ones from the ones

188
00:07:27,948 --> 00:07:30,491
who are going to push their
penis against your knee?

189
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:32,994
Excuse me?

190
00:07:33,036 --> 00:07:35,330
I know what she's talking about.

191
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:40,543
Uh, yeah.

192
00:07:40,585 --> 00:07:42,629
Well, we've got to find
a new pediatrician.

193
00:07:42,670 --> 00:07:44,298
Ross was getting sick last night

194
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:46,174
and I think Emma
may have caught it.

195
00:07:46,216 --> 00:07:47,801
Why don't you go see
Dr. Gettleman?

196
00:07:47,842 --> 00:07:49,261
Ross said he died.

197
00:07:49,303 --> 00:07:50,469
He didn't die.

198
00:07:50,511 --> 00:07:52,931
I just saw his daughter
last week.

199
00:07:52,973 --> 00:07:54,266
Said he was fine.

200
00:07:54,308 --> 00:07:57,018
Her, on the other hand,
botched Botox.

201
00:07:58,270 --> 00:07:59,729
Oh, great.

202
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:01,440
Well, then I'm going to take
Emma to see him.

203
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:02,941
I wonder why Ross
said that he died?

204
00:08:02,983 --> 00:08:04,609
Oh, maybe he was
getting him confused

205
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:05,986
with his childhood therapist.

206
00:08:06,027 --> 00:08:07,737
He saw a therapist?

207
00:08:07,779 --> 00:08:09,323
Mm-hmm. Yeah, he used to have
this recurring nightmare.

208
00:08:09,364 --> 00:08:10,407
Just really freaked him out.

209
00:08:10,449 --> 00:08:11,574
Why? What was it?

210
00:08:11,616 --> 00:08:14,535
That I was going to eat him.

211
00:08:27,924 --> 00:08:30,177
Mike!

212
00:08:31,345 --> 00:08:32,804
Yeah?

213
00:08:36,599 --> 00:08:38,310
Okay.

214
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:42,730
MIKE:
I got to tell you

215
00:08:42,772 --> 00:08:44,941
I can't believe
I'm doing this with you.

216
00:08:44,983 --> 00:08:47,610
Although, yeah, I did just get
out of a nine-year relationship.

217
00:08:47,652 --> 00:08:49,779
So, I guess I should be
open to taking some risks.

218
00:08:49,821 --> 00:08:51,031
Everything's going to be fine.

219
00:08:51,072 --> 00:08:52,491
Just follow my lead, okay?

220
00:08:52,531 --> 00:08:53,867
All you have to do is
pretend to be Mike.

221
00:08:53,908 --> 00:08:54,701
I am Mike.

222
00:08:54,742 --> 00:08:56,036
That a boy.

223
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:00,665
Okay, here they come.

224
00:09:00,707 --> 00:09:02,542
Hi, I'm Phoebe.
Hey, Phoebe, Mike.

225
00:09:02,583 --> 00:09:04,044
Nice to meet you.

226
00:09:04,085 --> 00:09:06,213
Joey, this is
Mary Ellen Jenkins.

227
00:09:06,254 --> 00:09:09,883
So, Mike, how do you and Joey
know each other anyway?

228
00:09:12,719 --> 00:09:14,888
How do I and Joey
know each other? Wow.

229
00:09:14,929 --> 00:09:18,225
If I had a nickel for every time
somebody's asked me that.

230
00:09:18,266 --> 00:09:20,477
( laughing )

231
00:09:20,519 --> 00:09:21,686
From-from school.

232
00:09:21,728 --> 00:09:22,937
Yeah, we met in college.

233
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:26,566
I mean high school.

234
00:09:29,277 --> 00:09:31,405
Wow, you guys go
way back, then.

235
00:09:31,446 --> 00:09:33,740
So, what are you
up to these days?

236
00:09:33,781 --> 00:09:34,824
Well, I'm a lawyer.

237
00:09:34,866 --> 00:09:38,245
Mike, Attorney-at-Law.

238
00:09:38,286 --> 00:09:39,871
Actually, I just gave up
my practice.

239
00:09:39,913 --> 00:09:41,248
What?

240
00:09:41,289 --> 00:09:45,419
That's the kind of thing
you usually run by me.

241
00:09:45,460 --> 00:09:47,670
Well, I always wanted
to play piano professionally

242
00:09:47,712 --> 00:09:49,923
and I figured if I don't
do this now I never will.

243
00:09:49,964 --> 00:09:51,466
Wow, that's great.

244
00:09:51,508 --> 00:09:53,260
I like that better
than the lawyer thing. So...

245
00:09:53,301 --> 00:09:56,596
Which is why I waited until
now to introduce you to Mike.

246
00:09:56,637 --> 00:09:58,723
I thought you thought
he was still a lawyer?

247
00:09:58,765 --> 00:10:00,517
No, no, no.
That's not what I meant.

248
00:10:00,559 --> 00:10:02,102
Let's-let's get you a cocktail.

249
00:10:02,143 --> 00:10:04,396
( snaps his fingers )

250
00:10:05,646 --> 00:10:06,606
Hey.
Hey.

251
00:10:06,647 --> 00:10:08,149
What are you doing?

252
00:10:08,191 --> 00:10:10,693
Looking for restaurant jobs
for you in Tulsa.

253
00:10:10,735 --> 00:10:11,903
Oh, that's so sweet.

254
00:10:11,945 --> 00:10:13,238
Did you find anything?

255
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:14,448
Slim Pickins'.

256
00:10:14,489 --> 00:10:15,823
Nothing, huh?

257
00:10:15,865 --> 00:10:17,325
No. Slim Pickins'.

258
00:10:17,367 --> 00:10:19,160
It's a barbecue joint.

259
00:10:19,202 --> 00:10:20,870
They're looking for a cook.

260
00:10:20,912 --> 00:10:23,498
Actually "cook" may be
a bit of a stretch.

261
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:26,543
They're looking for somebody
to shovel mesquite.

262
00:10:28,211 --> 00:10:30,422
Slim Pickins'.
That is so cheesy.

263
00:10:30,464 --> 00:10:33,467
So Cheesy also
has an opening.

264
00:10:33,508 --> 00:10:34,593
Well, honey, that's okay.

265
00:10:34,634 --> 00:10:35,927
I actually know
this woman, Nancy

266
00:10:35,969 --> 00:10:37,678
who's a-- she's
a restaurant biz headhunter.

267
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:39,264
Maybe she'll
know something.

268
00:10:39,306 --> 00:10:42,476
Can I just say how much I
appreciate you coming with me.

269
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:44,060
When we get to Tulsa

270
00:10:44,102 --> 00:10:46,146
I am taking you for a
great dinner at Slim Pickins'.

271
00:10:47,564 --> 00:10:49,149
So Cheesy?

272
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:54,028
Whole Hog?

273
00:10:54,070 --> 00:10:56,323
It's going to be tough
to keep kosher in Tulsa?

274
00:11:01,661 --> 00:11:03,121
Hi, Nancy?

275
00:11:03,163 --> 00:11:04,831
Hi, it's Monica Geller.

276
00:11:04,872 --> 00:11:06,458
I'm good.

277
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:10,253
Listen, I'm looking for a job
in Tulsa.

278
00:11:10,295 --> 00:11:13,798
Well, my husband's
been relocated.

279
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:16,801
Because I love him.

280
00:11:16,843 --> 00:11:20,054
No, no, I don't want a job
in New York.

281
00:11:20,096 --> 00:11:21,515
Javu's looking?

282
00:11:21,556 --> 00:11:23,266
Oh, my God.

283
00:11:23,308 --> 00:11:26,685
He asked for me personally?
Oh, my God!

284
00:11:26,727 --> 00:11:31,566
Oh, wow, this is really,
really flattering.

285
00:11:31,608 --> 00:11:34,611
But, um, I'm moving to Tulsa.

286
00:11:34,653 --> 00:11:36,904
Yeah, so, just if you
would tell Javu...

287
00:11:36,946 --> 00:11:38,448
I'll take it!

288
00:11:40,659 --> 00:11:42,661
Hi, my name's
Rachel Green.

289
00:11:42,701 --> 00:11:44,204
I have an appointment for Emma.

290
00:11:44,245 --> 00:11:46,414
Dr. Gettleman is finishing up
with a patient.

291
00:11:46,456 --> 00:11:48,166
He should be out shortly.
Okay, thank you.

292
00:11:48,208 --> 00:11:50,335
I think you just have a cold.

293
00:11:50,377 --> 00:11:51,878
It's definitely not strep.

294
00:11:51,919 --> 00:11:53,421
Thanks, doctor.

295
00:11:56,174 --> 00:11:57,800
Would you like
a lollipop?

296
00:11:57,842 --> 00:11:59,010
Even have to ask?

297
00:12:04,683 --> 00:12:07,644
He is alive!

298
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:14,942
You know,
it's so surprising

299
00:12:14,984 --> 00:12:17,445
that you and Joey
have known each
other for so long

300
00:12:17,487 --> 00:12:19,113
and I've never
heard about you.

301
00:12:19,155 --> 00:12:21,157
Oh, that's... that's because

302
00:12:21,199 --> 00:12:23,451
we had a bit of a falling out.

303
00:12:23,493 --> 00:12:26,913
Mike... hit my mom
with a car.

304
00:12:28,415 --> 00:12:30,500
No, I didn't.

305
00:12:30,542 --> 00:12:31,750
It's okay, Mike.

306
00:12:31,792 --> 00:12:33,336
I have forgiven you.

307
00:12:33,378 --> 00:12:35,796
And now we're friends again,
and everything's great.

308
00:12:35,838 --> 00:12:37,674
Wait. Is your mom okay?

309
00:12:37,716 --> 00:12:40,134
Please, we're trying
to have a conversation.

310
00:12:42,053 --> 00:12:45,098
Wow, you're a lot nicer
on Days of Our Lives.

311
00:12:45,139 --> 00:12:46,349
Days of Our Lives.

312
00:12:46,391 --> 00:12:47,808
That's why you look
so familiar.

313
00:12:47,850 --> 00:12:48,435
What?

314
00:12:48,476 --> 00:12:49,102
What?

315
00:12:49,143 --> 00:12:50,061
What?

316
00:12:51,854 --> 00:12:54,148
Do you not know
each other?

317
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:55,692
( chuckling )

318
00:12:55,734 --> 00:12:57,277
Of course we do.

319
00:12:57,318 --> 00:12:58,903
Mike is playing a game

320
00:12:58,945 --> 00:13:01,531
that we used to play
in high school.

321
00:13:01,573 --> 00:13:03,991
Yeah, where we pretend
we don't know each other.

322
00:13:04,033 --> 00:13:05,577
We played all kinds of games.

323
00:13:05,619 --> 00:13:08,121
Hey, you remember that one
where I punch you in the face

324
00:13:08,162 --> 00:13:11,040
for not being cool?

325
00:13:11,082 --> 00:13:13,000
Mike, let me
ask you something.

326
00:13:13,042 --> 00:13:15,002
How many sisters
does Joey have?

327
00:13:15,044 --> 00:13:16,129
Six.

328
00:13:16,170 --> 00:13:18,923
What are you doing?
I said seven!

329
00:13:18,965 --> 00:13:21,092
Oh!

330
00:13:21,134 --> 00:13:24,512
Joey, why did you set me up
with a stranger?

331
00:13:24,554 --> 00:13:28,099
Because I... I forgot
about our date.

332
00:13:28,141 --> 00:13:29,434
I'm so sorry.

333
00:13:29,476 --> 00:13:30,727
I'm sorry, too.

334
00:13:30,769 --> 00:13:31,978
And just to be clear

335
00:13:32,019 --> 00:13:33,980
I didn't hit his mother
with a car.

336
00:13:34,021 --> 00:13:35,898
You are unbelievable.

337
00:13:35,940 --> 00:13:40,069
I spent so much time
finding the perfect
girl for you, you know?

338
00:13:40,111 --> 00:13:42,489
Mary Ellen is really
smart and cute and loose.

339
00:13:42,530 --> 00:13:44,157
Hey!

340
00:13:44,198 --> 00:13:45,742
Oh, who are you kidding?

341
00:13:51,498 --> 00:13:54,917
You just find some guy
off the street for me?

342
00:13:54,959 --> 00:13:56,919
God, this is humiliating.

343
00:13:56,961 --> 00:13:58,546
Phoebe, I'm
so sorry.

344
00:13:58,588 --> 00:14:00,340
Hey, look, if you don't
like this guy

345
00:14:00,381 --> 00:14:01,758
I can find you a better one.

346
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:04,385
Mike? Mike?

347
00:14:04,427 --> 00:14:06,304
I'm out of here.

348
00:14:06,346 --> 00:14:08,389
It was nice
meeting you.

349
00:14:08,431 --> 00:14:10,141
You're leaving,
too?

350
00:14:10,183 --> 00:14:12,101
I'll stay if you can
tell me my name.

351
00:14:12,143 --> 00:14:13,520
Have a good night.

352
00:14:16,648 --> 00:14:18,274
Honey, we're
leaving tomorrow

353
00:14:18,316 --> 00:14:20,777
and you've still got
a lot of packing to do.

354
00:14:20,819 --> 00:14:21,944
You're right.

355
00:14:21,986 --> 00:14:24,614
Maybe I shouldn't go.

356
00:14:24,656 --> 00:14:26,407
What?

357
00:14:26,449 --> 00:14:30,203
Um... So Nancy told me about
this... this job

358
00:14:30,244 --> 00:14:32,539
at this great restaurant,
Javu.

359
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:35,082
But it's just a little
outside of Tulsa.

360
00:14:35,124 --> 00:14:36,584
Well, how far outside?

361
00:14:36,626 --> 00:14:39,003
Manhattan.

362
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:43,132
And, uh, you're
thinking of taking it?

363
00:14:45,844 --> 00:14:48,680
So, before you said
that being with me

364
00:14:48,722 --> 00:14:51,015
was more important
than any job

365
00:14:51,057 --> 00:14:54,644
but I guess now it's
old job... me... new job.

366
00:14:56,187 --> 00:14:58,105
I'm going to miss this hand.

367
00:15:00,734 --> 00:15:02,151
Okay, I know this
is a lot to ask

368
00:15:02,193 --> 00:15:03,570
but, oh, my God, Chandler

369
00:15:03,611 --> 00:15:05,112
this is a once-in-a-lifetime
opportunity.

370
00:15:05,154 --> 00:15:06,447
What happened to

371
00:15:06,489 --> 00:15:08,241
"you can't live without me
four days a week"?

372
00:15:08,282 --> 00:15:10,076
Well, if you really
think about it

373
00:15:10,117 --> 00:15:12,245
I mean four days
is just not that long.

374
00:15:12,286 --> 00:15:14,288
I mean...

375
00:15:14,330 --> 00:15:16,123
I see you Monday
before you go to work

376
00:15:16,165 --> 00:15:18,334
and then I see you
Thursday when you get back

377
00:15:18,376 --> 00:15:20,002
and I always work late
on Tuesdays

378
00:15:20,044 --> 00:15:21,671
so really if you
think about it...

379
00:15:21,713 --> 00:15:22,921
it's really just one day.

380
00:15:22,963 --> 00:15:25,633
And, um, if we can't
make it one day

381
00:15:25,675 --> 00:15:28,094
we got real problems,
my friend.

382
00:15:31,431 --> 00:15:34,141
I think you should take the job.

383
00:15:34,183 --> 00:15:35,476
Really?

384
00:15:35,518 --> 00:15:37,520
Yeah. I know it must be
important to you

385
00:15:37,562 --> 00:15:39,731
when you start chattering
like a monkey.

386
00:15:41,399 --> 00:15:44,903
That's the nicest thing
anybody's ever said to me.

387
00:15:47,238 --> 00:15:49,532
Hey.

388
00:15:49,574 --> 00:15:51,743
How was the pediatrician?

389
00:15:51,785 --> 00:15:53,661
Oh, I really
liked him.

390
00:15:53,703 --> 00:15:57,707
Yeah, yeah, it was really,
really, really good.

391
00:15:57,749 --> 00:15:59,166
You promised you wouldn't
say anything.

392
00:15:59,208 --> 00:16:01,085
I know.

393
00:16:01,127 --> 00:16:03,129
Ross still sees
his pediatrician!

394
00:16:05,632 --> 00:16:07,258
I don't care.

395
00:16:09,385 --> 00:16:10,595
Are you serious?

396
00:16:10,637 --> 00:16:12,764
You still see
Dr. Gettleman?

397
00:16:12,806 --> 00:16:14,849
He is a brilliant
diagnostician.

398
00:16:14,891 --> 00:16:16,893
Diagnostician or...

399
00:16:16,935 --> 00:16:18,812
boo-boo fixer?

400
00:16:18,853 --> 00:16:22,231
Ross, seriously, you've got
to go to an appropriate doctor.

401
00:16:22,273 --> 00:16:23,357
Why? Why?

402
00:16:23,399 --> 00:16:24,943
I know it's a little weird

403
00:16:24,983 --> 00:16:27,153
but, hey, he is a great doctor,
okay?

404
00:16:27,194 --> 00:16:28,863
He knows my medical history

405
00:16:28,905 --> 00:16:31,908
and every time I go in there
he makes a big deal.

406
00:16:31,950 --> 00:16:34,243
"Oh, look,
it's my favorite patient."

407
00:16:34,285 --> 00:16:35,662
Does he say that

408
00:16:35,703 --> 00:16:38,080
before he sticks his thermometer
in your tushie?

409
00:16:38,122 --> 00:16:39,666
Hey, I seem to remember

410
00:16:39,707 --> 00:16:42,919
someone bringing his
security blanket to college.

411
00:16:42,961 --> 00:16:44,587
That was not
a security blanket.

412
00:16:44,629 --> 00:16:45,964
That was a wall hanging.

413
00:16:46,005 --> 00:16:48,257
It didn't spend much time
on the wall!

414
00:16:58,142 --> 00:17:00,269
Oh, excuse me.

415
00:17:00,311 --> 00:17:01,938
Hi.

416
00:17:01,980 --> 00:17:03,356
I was hoping to run into you.

417
00:17:03,397 --> 00:17:04,440
Uh, can we talk?

418
00:17:04,482 --> 00:17:06,233
Sure.

419
00:17:06,275 --> 00:17:07,485
I'm sorry.

420
00:17:07,527 --> 00:17:09,779
Really, I'm
so embarrassed.

421
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:16,118
Really, I'm a pretty nice guy.

422
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:18,412
Just ask my parole officer.

423
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:23,417
Apparently, I'm not a funny guy.

424
00:17:26,044 --> 00:17:29,966
Well, why did you
go along with it?

425
00:17:30,008 --> 00:17:32,760
Because I was told
that I'd get a free dinner.

426
00:17:32,802 --> 00:17:34,679
Which I didn't.

427
00:17:34,721 --> 00:17:37,181
And that I'd meet
a pretty girl.

428
00:17:37,223 --> 00:17:38,683
Which I did.

429
00:17:40,727 --> 00:17:43,021
That's true.

430
00:17:43,062 --> 00:17:45,231
Well... is anything

431
00:17:45,272 --> 00:17:48,985
you told me about
yourself true?

432
00:17:49,027 --> 00:17:49,986
My name is Mike.

433
00:17:50,028 --> 00:17:51,320
And I do play piano.

434
00:17:51,362 --> 00:17:54,323
Oh, uh-huh, uh-huh.
Prove it.

435
00:17:54,365 --> 00:17:56,075
There isn't a piano here.

436
00:17:56,116 --> 00:17:59,328
That wouldn't stand in the way
of a true pianist.

437
00:17:59,370 --> 00:18:01,039
Okay.

438
00:18:17,972 --> 00:18:19,974
You are really good.

439
00:18:20,016 --> 00:18:21,768
Wow.

440
00:18:21,809 --> 00:18:24,395
I'm... I play a little
guitar myself.

441
00:18:24,437 --> 00:18:25,730
Really?

442
00:18:25,772 --> 00:18:26,814
Uh-huh.

443
00:18:26,856 --> 00:18:27,899
That's great.
Yeah.

444
00:18:27,941 --> 00:18:29,901
What kind of music
do you play?

445
00:18:29,943 --> 00:18:32,236
Well, um... like acoustic
folksy stuff, you know.

446
00:18:32,278 --> 00:18:35,031
But right now I'm working
on a couple Iron Maiden covers.

447
00:18:38,451 --> 00:18:40,745
Do you think
that maybe...

448
00:18:40,787 --> 00:18:44,040
sometime I could...?

449
00:18:44,082 --> 00:18:45,332
It's okay.

450
00:18:45,374 --> 00:18:46,626
Go ahead, ask me out.

451
00:18:46,667 --> 00:18:48,669
Okay.

452
00:18:48,711 --> 00:18:52,298
You think maybe sometime
I could take you out?

453
00:18:52,339 --> 00:18:54,634
Oh, you just caught me
off guard.

454
00:18:54,675 --> 00:18:57,595
Yes. That would
be nice, yeah.

455
00:18:57,637 --> 00:18:58,721
Look at this--

456
00:18:58,763 --> 00:19:00,389
my two best friends.

457
00:19:05,603 --> 00:19:06,896
Excuse me.

458
00:19:06,938 --> 00:19:08,773
I don't mean
to be a jerk

459
00:19:08,815 --> 00:19:12,234
but the baby with
the rash came in after me.

460
00:19:12,276 --> 00:19:15,154
The doctor will be
right with you, sir.

461
00:19:19,075 --> 00:19:21,786
Mommy, I can't
find Waldo.

462
00:19:21,828 --> 00:19:22,912
What? The circus?

463
00:19:22,954 --> 00:19:24,622
He's behind the elephant.

464
00:19:24,664 --> 00:19:26,707
Wow. So your child's
a big fan

465
00:19:26,749 --> 00:19:28,501
of the Waldo books, too?

466
00:19:30,128 --> 00:19:33,631
Yeah, that's how I know.

467
00:19:33,673 --> 00:19:35,633
I'm Ross, by the way.

468
00:19:35,675 --> 00:19:36,759
Hi. I'm Sally.

469
00:19:36,801 --> 00:19:37,760
Hi.

470
00:19:37,802 --> 00:19:39,178
So, no ring.

471
00:19:39,219 --> 00:19:40,680
Can I assume you're also
a single parent?

472
00:19:41,681 --> 00:19:43,057
I am a single parent.

473
00:19:43,099 --> 00:19:44,433
It's hard, isn't it?

474
00:19:44,475 --> 00:19:46,686
There's almost no time
for a social life.

475
00:19:46,727 --> 00:19:49,187
I mean, where are you going
to meet someone?

476
00:19:49,229 --> 00:19:51,941
Well, uh... let's say,
I don't know

477
00:19:51,983 --> 00:19:54,110
you met someone
in the...

478
00:19:54,152 --> 00:19:56,029
pediatrician's office.

479
00:19:57,113 --> 00:19:59,615
Rossie, we're ready for you.

480
00:20:07,289 --> 00:20:09,249
Um, yeah.

481
00:20:11,502 --> 00:20:13,462
Come on, Ross Jr.

482
00:20:13,504 --> 00:20:15,798
It's time to go in.

483
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:25,016
Mommy, what's wrong
with that man?

484
00:20:25,058 --> 00:20:27,601
Hey, I helped you find Waldo!

485
00:20:33,566 --> 00:20:34,942
( door opening )

486
00:20:34,984 --> 00:20:36,903
Oh, good, you haven't left yet.

487
00:20:36,944 --> 00:20:38,320
Where have you been?

488
00:20:38,362 --> 00:20:40,907
I got held up
at Dr. Gettleman's office.

489
00:20:40,948 --> 00:20:44,660
Uh, there was some guy
that, uh...

490
00:20:44,702 --> 00:20:46,704
freaked everybody out.

491
00:20:46,746 --> 00:20:48,206
Well, you got here
just in time.

492
00:20:48,246 --> 00:20:49,540
I really have to go, buddy.

493
00:20:49,582 --> 00:20:50,791
Oh, man.

494
00:20:50,833 --> 00:20:52,543
Honey, promise to call
me when you land?

495
00:20:52,585 --> 00:20:53,753
Of course
I will call you.

496
00:20:53,794 --> 00:20:54,754
I love you.

497
00:20:54,795 --> 00:20:56,089
I love you, too.

498
00:20:58,925 --> 00:21:00,843
Okay, whoa, whoa.
whoa, whoa.

499
00:21:00,885 --> 00:21:02,344
Whoa, watch
the tongue, people.

500
00:21:02,386 --> 00:21:03,596
We got a baby over here.

501
00:21:05,098 --> 00:21:06,640
Bye, Chandler.

502
00:21:06,682 --> 00:21:08,059
Oh, bye.

503
00:21:08,101 --> 00:21:09,351
Bye, honey.

504
00:21:09,393 --> 00:21:10,644
Bye.

505
00:21:14,732 --> 00:21:17,068
What's the matter, Joe?

506
00:21:17,110 --> 00:21:19,112
I'm mad at you
for leaving.

507
00:21:19,153 --> 00:21:21,906
You're nothing but
a big leaver.

508
00:21:23,241 --> 00:21:26,869
A big leaver with
a stupid suitcase.

509
00:21:26,911 --> 00:21:30,206
Any chance you're trying
to pick a fight

510
00:21:30,248 --> 00:21:31,999
to make all of this easier?

511
00:21:35,628 --> 00:21:37,797
Oh, dude, you see
right through me.

512
00:21:47,890 --> 00:21:50,935
Okay, well, bye, Mon...

513
00:21:50,977 --> 00:21:53,646
Bye, Ross, Rachel...

514
00:21:53,687 --> 00:21:55,106
Bye, Emma...

515
00:21:55,148 --> 00:21:57,357
Okay, bye-bye.

516
00:21:57,399 --> 00:21:58,442
Have a good trip.

517
00:21:58,484 --> 00:21:59,693
Okay.

518
00:21:59,735 --> 00:22:00,736
Oh, my God.

519
00:22:02,738 --> 00:22:04,531
Chandler...

520
00:22:04,573 --> 00:22:06,408
Wait.

521
00:22:07,451 --> 00:22:10,913
It goes, old job...

522
00:22:10,955 --> 00:22:12,873
new job...

523
00:22:12,915 --> 00:22:14,708
and you.

524
00:22:16,752 --> 00:22:18,879
This is just something
I have to do.

525
00:22:18,921 --> 00:22:21,507
I know.

526
00:22:21,548 --> 00:22:24,010
I love you so much.

527
00:22:24,051 --> 00:22:27,138
I know that, too.

528
00:22:32,143 --> 00:22:36,105
Captioned by
Media Access Group at WGBH
access.wgbh.org

529
00:22:38,356 --> 00:22:39,483
Don't worry.

530
00:22:39,525 --> 00:22:41,485
I'll be back before you know it.

531
00:22:41,527 --> 00:22:43,988
Yes, it will be the same.

532
00:22:44,030 --> 00:22:47,365
Because I know, that's how.

533
00:22:47,407 --> 00:22:48,659
I promise.

534
00:22:48,701 --> 00:22:50,161
You double promise?

535
00:22:52,288 --> 00:22:56,416
Well, call me
when you land.

536
00:22:56,458 --> 00:22:58,544
Can I talk now?

537
00:22:58,585 --> 00:22:59,879
Okay, bye.

538
00:22:59,920 --> 00:23:01,214
Joey?!

539
00:23:01,255 --> 00:23:02,506
He had to board.

